The central limit theorem gives an approximately normal sampling distribution, with
$\begin{align*}\sigma_{\bar{X}}&=\frac{30}{\sqrt{100}}\\&=3\text{ g}.\end{align*}$
(a)
$\begin{align*}z&=\frac{206-200}{3}\\&=2.\\P(\bar{X}>206)&\approx P(Z>2)\\&\approx\frac{1-0.95}{2}\\&=0.025.\end{align*}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=0.8cm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\path[use as bounding box] (-4.2,-1.3) rectangle (4.5,2.7);
\definecolor{regionblue}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[regionblue] (2,0) -- plot[domain=2:3.8,samples=40,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)}) -- (3.8,0) -- cycle;
\draw[black,thick] plot[domain=-3.8:3.8,samples=100,variable=\x] ({\x},{2.5*exp(-\x*\x/2)});
\draw[black,->] (-4,0) -- (4.3,0);
\draw[black] (0,0) -- (0,-0.12);
\draw[black] (2,0) -- (2,-0.12);
\path (0,-0.12) node[below] {$200$} (2,-0.12) node[below] {$206$} (4.1,-0.65) node[anchor=north east] {$\bar{x}\ (\mathrm{g})$};
\path (2.8,0.85) node {$\approx0.025$};
\draw[black] (2.8,0.6) -- (2.5,0.06);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
(b)
$\begin{align*}z&=\frac{197-200}{3}\\&=-1.\\P(\bar{X}>197)&\approx P(Z>-1)\\&\approx0.34+0.5\\&=0.84.\end{align*}$
The corresponding lower-tail probabilities are approximately $P(\bar{X}<206)=0.975$ and $P(\bar{X}<197)=0.16$.

The sampling distribution is approximately normal, with
$\begin{align*}\sigma_{\bar{X}}&=\frac{18}{\sqrt{81}}\\&=2.\end{align*}$
(a) The boundary $z$-scores are
$\begin{align*}z_{76}&=\frac{76-80}{2}=-2,\\z_{84}&=\frac{84-80}{2}=2.\\P(76<\bar{X}<84)&\approx P(-2<Z<2)\\&\approx0.95.\end{align*}$
(b) The lower boundary now has $z_{78}=\frac{78-80}{2}=-1$.
$\begin{align*}P(78<\bar{X}<84)&\approx P(-1<Z<2)\\&\approx0.34+0.475\\&=0.815.\end{align*}$
